==== Opening the case ====

{{:media:zyxel:nr7101:red_img_2152.jpg?400|NR7101 plastic cover off}}
{{:media:zyxel:nr7101:red_dsc_3822.jpg?400|NR7101 antenna and main PCBs}}
{{:media:zyxel:nr7101:red_img_2164.jpg?400|NR7101 heat transfer}}

There is no glue or plastic tabs holding the router together.  Only screws. The case is one of the easiest ones to open, despite the IP68 certification.  There is no need to disconnect any antenna cables either. But if you do, then I recommend taking lots of pictures first, since there is an awfull lot of them...

  * Remove the 2 screws securing the SIM/button/LED cover and pull off the WLAN button with a knife or similar shar object.
  * Remove the 12 screws on the backside, along the edge of the case.
  * Split the plastic antenna cover from the metal backplate with the PCBs.  The GPS antenna is fixed to the cover, so be careful with the cable
  * Remove the 4 screws securing the antenna PCB to the main PCB

The main PCB can be removed from the backplate by removing 4 screws. This is normally not necessary and might reduce the efficiency of the thermal pads connecting to the backplate unless you replace them.  But the pads are not glued and will not be damaged by removal, so reuse is most likely possible.


==== Serial ====
-> [[docs:techref:hardware:port.serial]] general information about the serial port, serial port cable, etc.


A 3.3V TTL UART is located on the populated 5 pin header J5 in the upper left corner of the main PCB (down is the side with ethernet port, buttons and SIM slots).

{{:media:zyxel:nr7101:red_dsc_3826.jpg?800|NR7101 serial}}

Pinout:
     [o] GND (black)
     [ ] key - no pin
     [o] RX (orange)
     [o] TX (red)
     [o] 3.3V Vcc (brown)

3.3V should **not** be connected to USB TTL UART adapters, as per [[docs:techref:hardware:port.serial]], but is available if you want to connect to a Bluetooth TTL UART adapter as shown in the picture.  Bluetooth allows console connections with all gaskets in place, maintaining IP68, but has some obvious security implications. 

It is also possible to route a serial or USB cable through the cable gland used by the ethernet cable, either using 2 thinner cables or a cable with more than 4 pairs. A single multipair round cable is recommended if the router is mounted outdoors.

^ Serial connection parameters\\ for ZyXEL NR7101 | 57600, 8N1 |

